  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 140 PM CST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nPeriods of Rain Through Thursday...\nAn active period of weather will move into the region today as a\nslow moving low pressure system arrives in the region. This system\nwill bring periods of light to moderate rainfall, gusty winds and\na few rumbles of thunder this evening through Thursday. The\ninitial push of warmer air and moisture arrives this afternoon and\nevening, bringing PWATS to near their climatological maximum for\nthe overnight period and much of Tuesday. Therefore expect the\ninitial period of moderate rainfall to occur tonight and again on\nTuesday. The higher moisture will get cutoff late Tuesday, as\nwinds shift westwards, so rainfall amounts are expected to be\nlower for Wednesday and Thursday. Thunder may mix in at times\ntonight, Tuesday afternoon, and again Wednesday afternoon as\nsoundings do indicate potential for some modest elevated\ninstability around a couple hundred J/kg. No strong or severe\nstorms are expected however.\n\nDespite the prolonged period of rain this week, a complete \nwashout is not expected, especially as showers through the period\nwill likely be on and off at times by Tuesday afternoon. The\nprobability of at least an inch of rainfall from tonight through\nWednesday evening is around 60-90 percent for most of central, \nand north- central Wisconsin, and around 40-70 percent for east- \ncentral Wisconsin including much of the Fox Valley. The \nprobability of at least 2 inches remains a more broad 20-50% \nchance across the region, mainly west and north of the Fox Valley.\nDepending on which areas see some convective elements mix in each\nafternoon (i.e. thunder), these areas may also get closer to the\ntwo inch rainfall mark. A few lighter showers may linger late\nWednesday night into through much of Thursday, but remaining qpf\nremains relatively light and showers are more likely to see breaks\nbetween periods of light rainfall. Dry conditions do finally\nreturn Friday and Saturday but another short-wave may bring a few\nshowers back on Sunday.\n\nTemperatures...\nThe surge of warm and moist air will keep high temperatures above\nnormal the next couple of days, with 60s forecast for much of the\nregion. The influx of warmer air may also bring some new record\nhigh minimum temperatures for some sites on Tuesday. A slow drop \nis then expected for the latter part of the week with highs \ngetting back to the upper 40s to lower 50s.",
